  "narrative": "After validation, the EGFR amplification main-effect claim is not supported. Discovery R3 was borderline in the favorable direction, with a signed estimate of about 0.025 and a 95% interval just crossing zero; validation R13 was near zero, signed about 0.0015, and its wider independent interval does not corroborate a clinically meaningful EGFR-dependency effect. I therefore reject and close the general EGFR main-effect claim, while keeping the separate TP53-restricted EGFR comparison active because context-specific effects are not excluded by a failed general main-effect test.\n\nThe strongest supported dataset-specific associations are H5, H6, H8, and the accepted reversal H16. SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H5, R5), MSI-high is associated with much stronger WRN dependency (H6, R6), and TP53 loss is associated with much weaker USP7 dependency (H8, R8). KEAP1 loss, contrary to the pre-specified expectation in H9, is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency and is accepted as a dataset-specific reversal in H16 (R9). These effects are far beyond the 10% relative-effect threshold, have intervals excluding zero in the supported direction, and have adequate cell counts. They should be interpreted as associations in this supplied 2000-cell lung line dataset, not as proof of causality or evidence of generalizability across other datasets or biological settings.\n\nNegative or unsupported findings include H2, H4, H7, H10, H11 direct, H12, H9 in its original direction, and H3 after validation. H2, H4, H7, H10, H11 direct, and H12 had negligible or interval-centered-null effects. H9 had a large opposite effect, which is formalized as H16. The original EGFR main effect remains a context-dependent uncertainty rather than an accepted association because H14 is still pending.\n\nUnresolved active work should focus on whether the robust associations are stable across biological and technical strata, and on completing coverage of the remaining outcomes. The most informative next analyses are the already registered interactions and remaining main effects: KRAS by TP53-loss interaction (H13), EGFR amplicon restriction in TP53-loss-negative cells (H14), SMARCA2 context interactions (H15 and newly registered TP53-loss and subtype interactions), remaining driver effects on KIF18A, RIT1, STAT3, and TMED10 (H17 to H20), expression-stratified MYC effects on RPL11 (H21), and the newly registered USP7 interaction by MYC amplification. If any of these reveal a consistent at-least-10% relative effect with adequate cells, that claim deserves validation; otherwise, further exploration should be curtailed in favor of the strongest replicated dataset-specific signals.",
